claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:


[...]

> +- Logins and their rights, enforced by Dovecot's ACL:
> +  - =pat@p7x.dev= and =mbsync= must give the password in
> +    =imap-users=, and a wrong one fails. They may do anything to the
> +    list: set flags, append, copy, move, and expunge.
> +  - Any other user name logs in with any password. It may list,
> +    fetch, search, and IDLE, and nothing else. Dovecot rejects its
> +    attempts to set flags, append, copy into a mailbox, or expunge.

That's an interesting design choice, and I don't disagree with it... we
are making a mailing list, after all. However, the ACL behavior needs to
be documented prominently.

Also is there any precedent for using IMAP folders in this way?


[...]

> +- notmuch indexes the mail root. Its pre-new hook runs mbsync first,
> +  so one =notmuch new= syncs and indexes, and a failed sync stops it

hrm... I don't think I want a network issue to stop notmuch from
indexing. What's the point of this? Is it to not have a stale cache?

Let's talk through this a bit. I'm not sure this sync needs to happen at
all. I'm fine with notmuch new just indexing what's new in the dir, not
triggering a fetch.

If I want a fetch, I can do it a different way.


[...]

> +- msmtp posts through 127.0.0.1:2525 with no auth and no TLS, from
> +  =pat@p7x.dev= on beastie and =claude@p7x.dev= on lab-claude. When
> +  the server refuses any recipient or the message, msmtp posts
> +  nothing and exits non-zero with the server's reply, so the whole
> +  message is abandoned.

Why no auth? I guess IMAP needs auth because users have different
permissions, whereas with SMTP everyone has the same permission to
deliver?


[...]

> +- On beastie, notmuch-emacs reads the mail root and sends through
> +  msmtp. A sent message is also saved by Fcc into
> +  =~/mail/pat@p7x.dev/sent=, beside the list's folders and not synced,
> +  so it shows as sent before the next sync brings it back from the
> +  list. Emacs does not refer to the jj maildir.

What's the behavior of this once it syncs? Will there be duplicate
message? Or they have the same ID, so it just gets a different tag?
